 # LOD Selection and Culling
 
-Also called: Level-of-Detail Selection, Hierarchical Culling, Multi-resolution Approximation
+Also called: Level-of-Detail Selection, Hierarchical Culling, Adaptive Resolution
 
 ## Overview
-LOD Selection and Culling is the family of methods that dynamically determine the appropriate spatial resolution for rendering volumetric data based on view-dependent metrics (such as distance, projected size, or screen footprint) and hardware constraints [S1][S2, unofficial]. It solves the performance bottleneck of rendering large volumetric datasets by discarding invisible subregions and rendering visible regions at resolutions proportional to their visual importance [S1][S2, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Mesh Simplification](1032-mesh-simplification.md) by operating on volumetric primitives (voxels, bricks, splats) rather than surface meshes, and from [Wavelet-based Volume Rendering](1052-wavelet-based-volume-rendering.md) by focusing on the selection logic and culling of pre-existing or generated resolution levels rather than the decomposition mechanism itself.
+LOD Selection and Culling determines which resolution level of a multiresolution volume hierarchy to render for a given view or spatial region, balancing visual fidelity against memory and performance constraints [S1][S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of rendering large volumetric datasets that exceed hardware budgets by dynamically discarding invisible subregions (culling) and selecting coarse approximations for distant or less important areas (LOD selection). It differs from sibling methods like [Mesh Simplification](1032-mesh-simplification.md) by operating on volumetric data structures (bricks, octrees, splats) rather than polygonal meshes, and from [Wavelet-based Volume Rendering](1052-wavelet-based-volume-rendering.md) by focusing on the selection logic and data management rather than the specific transform decomposition itself.
 
