 ## Background
-LOD selection and culling are fundamental techniques in volume rendering, originating from the need to handle the computational complexity of raycasting and texture-based rendering on large datasets. Early work focused on hierarchical structures like octrees and bricks to enable frustum culling and resolution selection based on distance [S1][S2, unofficial]. The introduction of GPU-based rendering accelerated the adoption of dynamic LOD calculation, allowing for real-time adjustments based on screen-space error and projected size [S1][S2, unofficial]. Recent developments include temporal coherence to prevent flickering [S1], metadata-driven selection using histogram spectra for out-of-core volumes [S1], and hybrid approaches that combine geometric and appearance-based metrics [S2, unofficial].
+The concept of Level of Detail (LOD) in volume rendering originated from the need to handle the computational complexity of raycasting large datasets interactively. Early work focused on hierarchical data structures like octrees and bricks to enable efficient traversal and culling [S1][S2, unofficial]. The integration of LOD with streaming architectures allowed for the visualization of terabyte-scale datasets by dynamically loading only the necessary resolution levels [S1]. Recent developments have extended LOD selection to GPU-based dynamic calculation for terrain and large-scale scenes, incorporating factors like terrain ruggedness to maintain visual quality [S1]. Additionally, error-driven LOD assignment algorithms have been developed to optimize memory usage by minimizing visual error density per byte [S1].
 
